export const HL1={title:'The event, the test and the portfolio',hook:'Healthy Living combines what you know with what you do. You take a 50-question test on nutrition, physical activity, mental health, wellness across the lifespan and disease prevention—and you spend the year working toward a personal healthy living goal, documenting your journey in a portfolio you present to judges. It is a chance to practice what health professionals ask patients to do every day.',objectives:['Describe the event’s rounds, test plan and scoring.','Explain the personal SMART goal and portfolio requirements.','Identify the official NASM course as the test reference.','Plan a year of study and goal work.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Healthy Living is a **solo event** in the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions. **Round One** is a **50-item multiple-choice test in 60 minutes**, with no verbal time announcements. Top scorers advance to **Round Two**, a **five-minute presentation** (new for 2026–27) using a **printed portfolio** that documents a personal healthy living goal. Test and presentation scores are added for final results, and a tie goes to the higher test score.',
'Bring photo ID, two #2 pencils with erasers for the test and your printed portfolio. Notecards are allowed in Round Two; electronic notes are not shown to judges, and only the portfolio may be shown.',
'The event is sponsored by the **National Academy of Sports Medicine (NASM)**, which created a HOSA Healthy Living course.',
"Because only top test scorers advance, the test is the gateway to Round Two. A strong portfolio cannot be presented unless you first score well on the 50 questions.",
{t:'table',caption:'Round One test plan',head:['Area','Share'],rows:[['Nutrition','25%'],['Physical activity','25%'],['Wellness through the lifespan','20%'],['Disease prevention','20%'],['Mental health','10%']]}]],
['The official reference',[
'The test follows selected units of the **HOSA Healthy Living Course created by NASM**, which includes PDFs, articles, videos and blogs organized by the test plan. An additional NASM webinar on health and wellness careers is available through HOSA webinars.',
'Sample items show the course’s style: when the first protein or energy bar was produced in the United States, what percentage of the body’s glucose the brain uses and which spice may help reduce high blood sugar. Questions may be detailed, so study the course materials closely, not just general health knowledge.',
'Take notes from every unit and turn facts into flashcards.',
"Study each unit in proportion to the test plan. Nutrition and physical activity together make up half the test, so they deserve about half your study time.",
chk('What is the official reference for the Healthy Living test?',['Selected units of the HOSA Healthy Living Course created by NASM','Any fitness magazine','A single textbook chosen by the competitor','Social media posts'],'NASM course.')]],
['The SMART goal',[
'Each competitor sets **one personal healthy living goal** in any **dimension of wellness**—physical, emotional, social, intellectual and more—focused on **building or maintaining healthy behaviors** or **avoiding risky behaviors**. The goal must follow the **SMART** formula: **Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Realistic and Timely**.',
'Before starting, analyze your current health and **consult a licensed health practitioner**. The goal runs from **July 1, 2026, to May 15, 2027**. If you advance from state to ILC, the goal does not change, but you continue working and may update the portfolio.',
'HOSA does not encourage fad diets or programs not directed by a licensed provider.',
"Choose a goal you genuinely want to achieve. Judges notice when competitors are personally invested, and real motivation makes it easier to stay consistent for months.",
sort('Fits the SMART goal rules?',['Fits','Does not fit'],[['Drink 64 oz of water daily by May, tracked on a calendar',0],['“Be healthier”',1],['Run a mile in under 8 minutes by June, tracked in a fitness log',0],['A crash diet from a social media trend',1],['Meditate 10 minutes each morning to reduce stress headaches',0]])]],
['The portfolio',[
'The portfolio documents your goal and journey. Required items: **title page** (event name, your name and age, chapter, division, school, chartered association and specific goal); a **baseline health assessment** relevant to the goal, completed before the goal starts; a **parent/guardian/advisor permission form** for competitors 17 or younger; **evidence of the journey**; and **reference page(s)** in APA style.',
'Beyond these, page count, photos and data are your choice. After Round Two, you leave the portfolio with judges, who score the title page, assessment, permission form and references.',
"Think of the portfolio as a story told in documents: where you started, what you did and what changed. Each page should help judges follow that story.",
flip('Portfolio requirements',[['Title page','Event, name, age, chapter, division, school, association, goal'],['Baseline assessment','Measured before the goal starts'],['Permission form','Required if 17 or younger'],['Evidence of journey','Logs, data, photos, letters'],['References','APA reference page'],['Other content','Your choice']])]],
['How Round Two is scored',[
'Judges score the **SMART goal**—follows the SMART formula, consistent with healthy living and challenging but attainable (10 each); **presentation content**—worked toward or maintained the goal, evidence shows healthy proof of progress and evidence provides clear, measurable baseline data (10 each), **context**—a confident verbal explanation of the journey (20) and **incorporation of the portfolio** (20); **delivery**—voice, stage presence and diction (5 each); and **portfolio requirements**—title page, baseline assessment, permission form and references (5 each).',
'Notice that context and portfolio use together are worth 40 points—how you tell and show your journey matters greatly.',
"The SMART goal items are scored on the goal itself, so a well-written goal earns points even before you show results. Spend time drafting and revising it with your advisor.",
match('Rubric item and points',[['SMART formula','10'],['Challenging but attainable','10'],['Context of the journey','20'],['Incorporation of the portfolio','20'],['Baseline health assessment','5'],['Voice','5']])]],
['A year-long plan',[
'**July–August:** complete a baseline health assessment, consult a licensed provider, write your SMART goal and get the permission form signed. **September–May:** work on your goal and document progress weekly. **All year:** study the NASM course units by test plan percentage. **Spring:** assemble the portfolio, take timed practice tests and rehearse the five-minute presentation.',
'Steady documentation is easier and more convincing than reconstructing evidence at the end.',
"Set a weekly reminder to update your portfolio evidence. Fifteen minutes each week prevents a stressful rush to reconstruct months of data before competition.",
order('Healthy Living timeline',['Baseline health assessment','Consult a licensed provider','Write the SMART goal','Get permission form signed','Work on and document the goal','Study NASM course units','Assemble portfolio and rehearse'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Getting started',{a:{text:'You want to start your goal before completing a baseline assessment. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Complete the baseline assessment first so progress can be measured','b','Correct.',true],['Skip the assessment','b','Baseline data are required and scored.',false]]},b:{text:'You are 16. What must your portfolio include?',choices:[['A signed parent/guardian/advisor permission form','c','Correct.',true],['Nothing extra','c','Competitors 17 or younger need the form.',false]]},c:{text:'You begin your goal with a baseline and signed permission.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Solo event (secondary and postsecondary): a 50-item, 60-minute test (nutrition 25%, physical activity 25%, lifespan 20%, disease prevention 20%, mental health 10%) from the NASM HOSA Healthy Living Course, then a 5-minute portfolio presentation for top scorers; scores are added.','Set one SMART personal healthy living goal (July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027) after analyzing your health and consulting a licensed practitioner; avoid fad diets or unsupervised programs.','The portfolio needs a title page, baseline health assessment, permission form (if 17 or younger), evidence of the journey and APA references; context (20) and portfolio incorporation (20) carry heavy weight.'],terms:[['SMART goal','A goal that is specific, measurable, attainable, realistic and timely.'],['Baseline health assessment','A measurement of health status before a goal begins.'],['Evidence of journey','Documentation showing progress toward the goal.']],mcqs:[q('How many items are on the Healthy Living test?','50','100','25','75','Test.'),q('Which area has the largest share of the test plan (tied)?','Nutrition and physical activity (25% each)','Mental health','Disease prevention','Wellness through the lifespan','Plan.'),q('Who created the official course?','NASM','The FDA','A social media influencer','The competitor','Reference.'),q('What does the T in SMART stand for?','Timely','Tough','Typical','Total','Formula.'),q('When does the goal period run?','July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027','Only during ILC','January–March','Any time','Rule.'),q('Who needs a permission form?','Competitors 17 or younger','All judges','Only postsecondary','No one','Rule.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','New for 2026–27.'),q('What may be shown to judges?','Only the portfolio','Slides','Props','A video','Rule.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Healthy Living event and how you would plan your goal, portfolio and test preparation.',rubric:[['Event',['50','60 minutes','test plan','nutrition','physical activity','5 minutes','portfolio']],['Goal',['smart','specific','measurable','licensed','baseline','july 1','may 15']],['Portfolio',['title page','baseline','permission','evidence','references']],['Plan',['nasm','study','document','weekly','rehearse','timeline']]],model:'Healthy Living is a solo event for secondary and postsecondary members. In Round One, I take a 50-item, 60-minute test based on the NASM HOSA Healthy Living Course, covering nutrition and physical activity at 25% each, wellness through the lifespan and disease prevention at 20% each and mental health at 10%. Top scorers advance to Round Two, a five-minute presentation of my printed portfolio documenting one personal healthy living goal, and my test and presentation scores are added. My goal must follow the SMART formula, focus on building healthy behaviors or avoiding risky ones and run from July 1, 2026, to May 15, 2027. In July and August, I would complete a baseline health assessment relevant to my goal, consult my doctor, write a goal such as running a mile in under eight minutes by May with a fitness log, and—because I am 16—get the parent/guardian/advisor permission form signed. From September through May, I would document progress weekly with logs, charts and photos as evidence of my journey. My portfolio would include a title page with the event name, my name and age, chapter, division, school, chartered association and goal, the baseline assessment, the permission form, evidence of my journey and an APA reference page. All year, I would study the NASM course units in proportion to the test plan, make flashcards and take timed practice tests, and in spring I would rehearse explaining my journey and using my portfolio as evidence.'},sources:[RULES,NASM]};

export const HL2={title:'Nutrition fundamentals',hook:'Nutrition is a quarter of the Healthy Living test, and it shapes energy, growth, mood and long-term disease risk. Every food choice combines macronutrients that provide energy and micronutrients that keep the body running. Understanding the basics lets you answer test questions and make better choices for your own goal.',objectives:['Describe the six classes of nutrients and their roles.','Calculate energy from macronutrients.','Explain key vitamins, minerals and hydration needs.','Apply the Dietary Guidelines and MyPlate.'],sections:[
['The six classes of nutrients',[
'The six nutrient classes are **carbohydrates**, **proteins**, **fats**, **vitamins**, **minerals** and **water**. **Macronutrients**—carbohydrates, proteins and fats—are needed in large amounts and provide **energy (calories)**. **Micronutrients**—vitamins and minerals—are needed in small amounts and regulate body processes. Water is essential for nearly every function.',
'**Carbohydrates** are the body’s main energy source, especially for the brain and muscles; **fiber** is a type of carbohydrate that supports digestion and heart health. **Proteins** build and repair tissues, enzymes and hormones and are made of **amino acids**, nine of which are **essential** (must come from food). **Fats** store energy, insulate, protect organs and help absorb vitamins A, D, E and K.',
"Complete proteins, which contain all nine essential amino acids, come from animal foods and some plant foods such as soy and quinoa. Combining plant proteins, such as beans and rice, also provides all essential amino acids.",
match('Nutrient and main role',[['Carbohydrates','Main energy source'],['Fiber','Supports digestion and heart health'],['Protein','Builds and repairs tissue'],['Fat','Stores energy and absorbs fat-soluble vitamins'],['Vitamins and minerals','Regulate body processes'],['Water','Temperature regulation and transport']])]],
['Energy from macronutrients',[
'**Carbohydrates and proteins provide about 4 calories per gram; fats provide about 9 calories per gram.** (Alcohol provides about 7 calories per gram but is not a nutrient.) Energy needs depend on age, sex, body size, growth and activity level.',
'You can calculate the calories in a food from its macronutrients—a common test skill.',
"Energy balance compares calories eaten with calories used. Over time, consistently eating more than you use leads to weight gain, while eating less leads to weight loss, though growth and health conditions affect this in teens.",
ex('Calculating calories','A snack bar has 20 g carbohydrate, 10 g protein and 8 g fat (invented for practice). How many calories?',['Carbohydrate: 20 × 4 = 80','Protein: 10 × 4 = 40','Fat: 8 × 9 = 72','Total: 80 + 40 + 72 = 192 calories'],'About 192 calories; fat supplies 72 ÷ 192 ≈ 38% (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Types of carbohydrates and fats',[
'**Simple carbohydrates** (sugars) digest quickly; **complex carbohydrates** (starches and fiber in whole grains, beans and vegetables) digest more slowly and provide steadier energy. **Added sugars** should be limited—the Dietary Guidelines recommend less than **10% of daily calories** from added sugars for people age 2 and older.',
'**Unsaturated fats** (olive oil, nuts, avocados, fish) support heart health; **omega-3 fatty acids** in fish are especially beneficial. **Saturated fats** (fatty meats, butter) should be limited to less than **10% of daily calories**, and **artificial trans fats** should be avoided.',
"Fiber recommendations for teens and adults are roughly 25 to 34 grams per day depending on age and sex, yet many people eat far less. Whole grains, beans, fruits and vegetables are the best sources.",
sort('Choose more often or limit?',['Choose more often','Limit'],[['Whole grains and beans',0],['Sugary drinks',1],['Olive oil and nuts',0],['Fried foods high in saturated fat',1],['Fatty fish such as salmon',0],['Candy and pastries',1]])]],
['Vitamins and minerals',[
'**Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K)** are stored in body fat; **water-soluble vitamins (B vitamins and C)** are not stored as much and must be replaced regularly. Key roles: vitamin A (vision), vitamin D (calcium absorption), vitamin C (immune function, wound healing), vitamin K (blood clotting), folate (cell growth, preventing neural tube defects) and B12 (red blood cells and nerves).',
'Key minerals: **calcium** (bones and teeth), **iron** (oxygen transport in hemoglobin), **potassium** (fluid balance and blood pressure), **sodium** (fluid balance—most people consume too much) and **zinc** (immune function). Deficiencies include iron-deficiency anemia and rickets from lack of vitamin D.',
"Most people get enough sodium—often too much—mainly from processed and restaurant foods rather than the salt shaker. The Dietary Guidelines recommend less than 2,300 milligrams per day for people 14 and older.",
match('Vitamin or mineral and function',[['Vitamin D','Calcium absorption'],['Vitamin C','Immune function and wound healing'],['Vitamin K','Blood clotting'],['Iron','Oxygen transport'],['Calcium','Bones and teeth'],['Folate','Cell growth and preventing neural tube defects']])]],
['Hydration',[
'Water makes up about **60% of adult body weight** and regulates temperature, transports nutrients, cushions joints and removes waste. Needs vary with activity, climate and body size. Thirst, urine color (pale yellow is a good sign) and how you feel help guide intake.',
'**Dehydration** can cause fatigue, headache, dizziness and reduced performance. During exercise, especially in heat, drink fluids before, during and after activity. Water is the best everyday drink; sugary drinks add calories without nutrients.',
"Caffeine in energy drinks and sodas can cause dehydration symptoms, poor sleep and a fast heart rate, and many health organizations advise teens to limit or avoid energy drinks.",
chk('Which is a sign of good hydration?',['Pale yellow urine','Dark amber urine','Constant headache','Dizziness'],'Urine color is a quick guide.')]],
['The Dietary Guidelines and MyPlate',[
'The **Dietary Guidelines for Americans** recommend a healthy dietary pattern at every life stage: emphasize **vegetables, fruits, whole grains, low-fat or fat-free dairy (or fortified alternatives), lean proteins and oils**, and limit **added sugars, saturated fat, sodium** and alcohol. **MyPlate** visualizes this with a plate of **fruits, vegetables, grains and protein** and a side of **dairy**, with the message to make half your plate fruits and vegetables and at least half your grains whole.',
'A **dietary pattern** over time matters more than any single food or meal.',
"The Dietary Guidelines emphasize that individual preferences, cultural traditions and budgets can all fit within a healthy pattern. There is no single “perfect” diet for everyone.",
order('Building a MyPlate meal',['Fill half the plate with fruits and vegetables','Add a whole grain','Add a lean protein','Include dairy or a fortified alternative','Choose water as the drink'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Nutrition drill',{a:{text:'A food has 12 g fat. How many calories come from fat? (Practice item.)',choices:[['108 calories','b','Correct—12 × 9.',true],['48 calories','b','Fat provides 9 calories per gram.',false]]},b:{text:'Which vitamin is needed for blood clotting?',choices:[['Vitamin K','c','Correct.',true],['Vitamin C','c','Vitamin C supports immunity and healing.',false]]},c:{text:'You add both facts to your flashcards.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Six nutrient classes: carbohydrates (main energy), proteins (build and repair; nine essential amino acids), fats (energy storage, fat-soluble vitamin absorption), vitamins, minerals and water.','Carbohydrates and proteins provide about 4 calories per gram, fat about 9; limit added sugars and saturated fat to under 10% of calories each and avoid trans fats; choose complex carbohydrates and unsaturated fats.','Know vitamin and mineral roles (A vision, D calcium absorption, C immunity, K clotting, iron oxygen transport, calcium bones), hydration signs and the Dietary Guidelines and MyPlate (half the plate fruits and vegetables).'],terms:[['Macronutrient','A nutrient needed in large amounts that provides energy.'],['Essential amino acid','An amino acid the body cannot make and must get from food.'],['Fat-soluble vitamin','A vitamin stored in body fat: A, D, E or K.']],mcqs:[q('How many calories per gram does fat provide?','About 9','About 4','About 7','About 2','Energy.'),q('How many calories per gram do carbohydrates provide?','About 4','About 9','About 1','About 12','Energy.'),q('Which vitamin helps the body absorb calcium?','Vitamin D','Vitamin C','Vitamin K','Vitamin B12','Function.'),q('Which mineral is part of hemoglobin?','Iron','Calcium','Sodium','Zinc','Oxygen transport.'),q('Which vitamins are fat-soluble?','A, D, E and K','B and C','C only','All of them','Classification.'),q('MyPlate recommends that half the plate be:','Fruits and vegetables','Protein','Grains','Dairy','MyPlate.'),q('Added sugars should be limited to less than what share of daily calories?','10%','50%','30%','1%','Guideline.'),q('Which is a sign of dehydration?','Dark urine and headache','Pale yellow urine','Steady energy','No thirst','Hydration.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the major nutrients, how to calculate calories and the key recommendations of the Dietary Guidelines and MyPlate.',rubric:[['Nutrients',['carbohydrates','protein','fat','vitamins','minerals','water','fiber']],['Calories',['4','9','gram','calculate','total']],['Micronutrients and hydration',['vitamin d','vitamin c','iron','calcium','fat-soluble','hydration']],['Guidelines',['dietary guidelines','myplate','half','whole grains','added sugars','saturated fat','sodium']]],model:'The six classes of nutrients are carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, minerals and water. Carbohydrates are the body’s main energy source, with fiber supporting digestion and heart health; proteins build and repair tissues and are made of amino acids, nine of which are essential; and fats store energy, protect organs and help absorb the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K. Carbohydrates and proteins provide about 4 calories per gram and fats about 9, so a snack bar with 20 grams of carbohydrate, 10 of protein and 8 of fat contains about 80 + 40 + 72 = 192 calories. Vitamins and minerals regulate body processes: vitamin D helps absorb calcium for bones, vitamin C supports immunity and healing, vitamin K supports blood clotting, iron carries oxygen in hemoglobin and potassium and sodium help regulate fluid balance. Water makes up about 60% of adult body weight, and pale yellow urine is a sign of good hydration, while headaches and dark urine can signal dehydration. The Dietary Guidelines for Americans recommend a healthy dietary pattern emphasizing vegetables, fruits, whole grains, low-fat dairy, lean proteins and healthy oils while limiting added sugars and saturated fat to less than 10% of calories each and reducing sodium. MyPlate shows this by making half the plate fruits and vegetables, at least half the grains whole and adding lean protein and dairy, with water as the best everyday drink.'},sources:[RULES,DGA,LABEL]};

export const HL3={title:'Nutrition in practice',hook:'Knowing nutrition facts is one thing; using them in a grocery store, cafeteria or locker room is another. This lesson covers reading the Nutrition Facts label, evaluating supplements and sports nutrition claims, eating for performance and building lasting habits—practical skills that also appear on the NASM-based test.',objectives:['Read and interpret the Nutrition Facts label.','Evaluate dietary supplements and nutrition claims.','Describe nutrition for physical activity and recovery.','Apply strategies for healthy eating habits.'],sections:[
['Reading the Nutrition Facts label',[
'Start with the **serving size** and **servings per container**—all numbers are per serving. Check **calories**, then nutrients to limit (**saturated fat, sodium, added sugars**) and nutrients to get enough of (**dietary fiber, vitamin D, calcium, iron, potassium**).',
'The **% Daily Value (%DV)** shows how much a serving contributes to a 2,000-calorie daily diet: **5% DV or less is low** and **20% DV or more is high**. The **ingredient list** is in order by weight, from most to least.',
"Compare similar products using the same serving size. Two cereals may list different serving sizes, so adjust the numbers before deciding which is healthier.",
ex('Using %DV','A cereal serving has 3% DV sodium and 22% DV fiber (invented for practice). Interpret.',['Sodium 3% DV: 5% or less = low','Fiber 22% DV: 20% or more = high','Conclusion: low in sodium and high in fiber','Check serving size before comparing products'],'Low sodium, high fiber per serving (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Dietary supplements',[
'**Dietary supplements** include vitamins, minerals, herbs, protein powders and other products. In the United States, supplements are regulated differently from drugs: manufacturers are responsible for safety and labeling, but products **do not need FDA approval before sale**. Quality varies, and some products contain ingredients not listed on the label.',
'Supplements can help in specific situations (for example, vitamin D or iron for a diagnosed deficiency), but most people can meet needs through food. Consult a health care provider before starting supplements, look for third-party testing seals and be wary of claims that sound too good to be true. The **NIH Office of Dietary Supplements** provides reliable information.',
"Some supplements interact with medicines or are harmful at high doses. Fat-soluble vitamins in particular can build up in the body and cause toxicity if taken in excess.",
"Protein powders are common supplements among teens. Most teens get enough protein from food, and high-protein supplements are rarely necessary unless recommended by a provider or dietitian.",
sort('Red flag or reasonable?',['Red flag','Reasonable'],[['“Burn fat fast with no exercise!”',0],['Taking iron after a provider diagnoses anemia',1],['“Miracle cure for all diseases”',0],['Choosing a product with a third-party testing seal',1],['Starting high-dose supplements without asking a provider',0]])]],
['Nutrition for activity',[
'Active people need enough **energy** to fuel training. **Carbohydrates** are the main fuel for moderate to intense exercise, so pre-exercise meals often include carbohydrates with moderate protein. **Protein** supports muscle repair, and spreading protein across meals is helpful. After exercise, a combination of carbohydrate and protein supports **recovery**.',
'**Hydration** is essential before, during and after activity. Sports drinks can help during long or intense exercise (often over an hour) or in heat, but water is enough for most shorter workouts.',
"Athletes in endurance sports may need more carbohydrates, while those focused on strength may need more protein, but most teens can meet needs with balanced meals and snacks.",
match('Timing and nutrition focus',[['2–3 hours before exercise','Balanced meal with carbohydrates'],['During long exercise','Fluids and possibly carbohydrates'],['After exercise','Carbohydrate plus protein for recovery'],['Throughout the day','Spread protein across meals'],['Hot weather','Extra fluids and electrolytes'],['Short workout','Water is usually enough']])]],
['Energy and protein bars',[
'**Protein and energy bars** became popular as convenient foods for athletes and busy people; the NASM course includes their history and uses. Bars can be useful for convenience, but many contain large amounts of **added sugar**, and some are closer to candy bars nutritionally.',
'Compare bars using the label: protein, fiber, added sugars, saturated fat and ingredient list. Whole foods such as fruit with nuts or yogurt often provide similar benefits with more nutrients.',
"Marketing terms such as “natural” or “energy” are not the same as nutritional quality. Always check the label rather than relying on front-of-package claims.",
chk('What should you check first when comparing two bars?',['Serving size and added sugars on the label','The color of the wrapper','The celebrity endorsement','The price only'],'Label literacy.')]],
['Functional foods and spices',[
'Some foods and spices are studied for potential health effects. The NASM course sample question asks about **cinnamon**, which may help lower high blood sugar (hyperglycemia). Other examples include **fiber-rich oats** (cholesterol), **fatty fish** (omega-3s for heart health), **yogurt** with live cultures (gut health) and **berries** (antioxidants).',
'These foods can be part of a healthy pattern, but they are not cures. Effects in studies are often modest, and a balanced diet matters most.',
"The NASM course may include specific facts about foods, spices and supplements, so note details carefully—test items can ask about particular examples rather than general principles.",
match('Food and studied benefit',[['Cinnamon','May help lower blood sugar'],['Oats','May lower LDL cholesterol'],['Fatty fish','Omega-3s for heart health'],['Yogurt with live cultures','Gut health'],['Berries','Antioxidants'],['Beans','Fiber and plant protein']])]],
['Building healthy eating habits',[
'Lasting change comes from **small, specific habits**: planning meals, keeping healthy snacks available, eating regular meals, drinking water, eating slowly and paying attention to hunger and fullness cues. Avoid **fad diets** that eliminate whole food groups or promise rapid weight loss—HOSA specifically discourages fad diets not directed by a licensed provider.',
'Track progress with simple tools such as a food or water log, which can also serve as evidence in your portfolio if nutrition is your goal area.',
"If nutrition is your goal area, consult a registered dietitian or licensed provider, who can help set safe targets and suggest realistic changes for your needs.",
order('Changing an eating habit',['Identify one habit to change','Set a specific, measurable target','Plan how and when you will do it','Track it daily','Review weekly and adjust','Celebrate progress'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Label and supplement drill',{a:{text:'A drink label shows 2 servings per bottle and 25 g added sugars per serving. You drink the whole bottle. (Practice item.) How much added sugar?',choices:[['50 g','b','Correct—2 × 25.',true],['25 g','b','Multiply by servings consumed.',false]]},b:{text:'A friend recommends a supplement promising to “melt fat overnight.” What do you do?',choices:[['Treat it as a red flag and ask a health care provider before using any supplement','c','Correct.',true],['Try it immediately','c','Extreme claims are warning signs.',false]]},c:{text:'You apply label and supplement skills wisely.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Read labels starting with serving size; 5% DV or less is low and 20% DV or more is high; limit saturated fat, sodium and added sugars; ingredients are listed by weight.','Supplements do not need FDA approval before sale; use them for specific needs with provider guidance, look for third-party testing and avoid too-good-to-be-true claims; NIH ODS is a reliable source.','Fuel activity with carbohydrates, protein for repair and carbohydrate plus protein after exercise; hydrate; compare bars by label; functional foods such as cinnamon may help but are not cures; build small, trackable habits and avoid fad diets.'],terms:[['% Daily Value','How much a serving contributes to daily nutrient needs.'],['Dietary supplement','A product such as vitamins or herbs taken to add to the diet.'],['Fad diet','A popular diet promising quick results, often without evidence.']],mcqs:[q('What should you check first on a Nutrition Facts label?','Serving size','Wrapper color','Brand name','Price','Label.'),q('A %DV of 20% or more is considered:','High','Low','Zero','Dangerous always','Rule.'),q('A %DV of 5% or less is considered:','Low','High','Moderate','Required','Rule.'),q('Do dietary supplements need FDA approval before sale?','No','Yes','Only vitamins','Only herbs','Regulation.'),q('Which spice may help reduce high blood sugar?','Cinnamon','Oregano','Basil','Paprika','Sample item.'),q('After exercise, recovery is supported by:','Carbohydrate plus protein','Only fat','Only caffeine','Skipping meals','Recovery.'),q('Ingredients on a label are listed by:','Weight, most to least','Alphabet','Color','Price','Label.'),q('Which is a red flag claim?','“Burn fat fast with no exercise!”','“Good source of fiber”','“Low sodium”','“Contains calcium”','Supplements.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to read a Nutrition Facts label, evaluate supplements and eat well for physical activity.',rubric:[['Labels',['serving size','calories','%dv','5%','20%','ingredients','added sugars']],['Supplements',['supplements','fda','approval','provider','third-party','claims','ods']],['Activity nutrition',['carbohydrates','protein','recovery','hydration','sports drinks']],['Habits',['small','specific','track','fad diets','licensed','log']]],model:'To read a Nutrition Facts label, I would first check the serving size and servings per container, since all values are per serving—if a bottle holds two servings with 25 grams of added sugar each, drinking it all means 50 grams. Then I would check calories, nutrients to limit such as saturated fat, sodium and added sugars, and nutrients to get enough of such as fiber, vitamin D, calcium, iron and potassium, using the % Daily Value: 5% or less is low and 20% or more is high. The ingredient list is in order by weight. For supplements, I know they do not need FDA approval before sale and quality varies, so I would use them only for specific needs, such as iron for diagnosed anemia, after talking with a health care provider, look for third-party testing seals, check the NIH Office of Dietary Supplements and treat claims like “melt fat overnight” as red flags. For physical activity, I would eat a balanced meal with carbohydrates two to three hours before exercise, spread protein across meals for muscle repair, have carbohydrate plus protein after workouts and hydrate before, during and after exercise, using sports drinks only for long or intense sessions in heat. Functional foods such as cinnamon, oats and fatty fish can support health but are not cures. Finally, I would build small, specific habits—planning meals, keeping healthy snacks and tracking water or food in a log—and avoid fad diets that HOSA discourages without a licensed provider’s direction.'},sources:[RULES,LABEL,ODS]};

export const HL4={title:'Physical activity guidelines and fitness',hook:'Physical activity is another quarter of the test—and one of the most powerful things people can do for their health. Regular activity strengthens the heart, muscles and bones, improves mood and sleep and lowers the risk of many chronic diseases. This lesson covers the national guidelines, the components of fitness and how to measure intensity.',objectives:['State the Physical Activity Guidelines for youth, adults and older adults.','Describe the components of health-related fitness.','Measure exercise intensity with heart rate and perceived exertion.','Explain the benefits of activity and risks of inactivity.'],sections:[
['National guidelines',[
'The **Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans** recommend that **children and adolescents (6–17)** get **60 minutes or more of moderate-to-vigorous activity daily**, including **vigorous** activity, **muscle-strengthening** and **bone-strengthening** activity on at least **3 days per week** each.',
'**Adults** should get **150–300 minutes of moderate** or **75–150 minutes of vigorous** aerobic activity per week (or an equivalent mix), plus **muscle-strengthening** activities on **2 or more days**. **Older adults** follow similar guidelines and add **balance training**. The key message: **move more and sit less**—some activity is better than none.',
"Muscle-strengthening activities for youth can include climbing, push-ups and resistance exercises, while bone-strengthening activities include running, jumping and other weight-bearing movements.",
"Sedentary behavior—sitting for long periods, including screen time—is a separate risk from not exercising. Standing up and moving for a few minutes every half hour helps offset it.",
{t:'table',caption:'Physical Activity Guidelines (summary)',head:['Group','Aerobic','Strength and other'],rows:[['Ages 6–17','60+ min daily, moderate to vigorous','Vigorous, muscle and bone strengthening 3+ days/week each'],['Adults','150–300 min moderate or 75–150 min vigorous weekly','Muscle strengthening 2+ days/week'],['Older adults','Same as adults as abilities allow','Add balance training']]}]],
['Components of health-related fitness',[
'Health-related fitness has five components: **cardiorespiratory endurance** (heart and lungs supplying oxygen during sustained activity), **muscular strength** (maximum force), **muscular endurance** (repeated contractions over time), **flexibility** (range of motion around joints) and **body composition** (proportion of fat and lean mass).',
'**Skill-related fitness** includes agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed—important for sports and daily function.',
"Cardiorespiratory endurance is especially linked to heart health, and it improves with regular aerobic activity such as brisk walking, cycling, swimming and running.",
match('Activity and fitness component',[['Jogging for 30 minutes','Cardiorespiratory endurance'],['One heavy squat','Muscular strength'],['Many push-ups in a row','Muscular endurance'],['Yoga stretches','Flexibility'],['Ratio of fat to lean mass','Body composition'],['Ladder drills','Agility (skill-related)']])]],
['Measuring intensity',[
'**Moderate** activity raises your heart rate and breathing—you can talk but not sing (brisk walking, easy cycling). **Vigorous** activity makes talking difficult (running, fast cycling, basketball). The **talk test** is a simple intensity check.',
'Estimated **maximum heart rate** is often calculated as **220 − age**. Moderate intensity is roughly **50–70%** and vigorous roughly **70–85%** of maximum. The **rating of perceived exertion (RPE)** scale lets people rate effort subjectively.',
"The 220 − age formula is only an estimate; individuals vary. Some people use heart rate monitors or fitness watches, but the talk test and perceived exertion remain useful and simple.",
"Vigorous activity generally provides about twice the benefit per minute of moderate activity, which is why 75 minutes of vigorous activity is considered roughly equal to 150 minutes of moderate activity.",
ex('Target heart rate','Estimate moderate and vigorous heart rate ranges for a 16-year-old (estimate for practice).',['Maximum ≈ 220 − 16 = 204 beats per minute','Moderate 50–70%: 102–143 bpm','Vigorous 70–85%: 143–173 bpm','Use the talk test to confirm'],'Moderate about 102–143 bpm; vigorous about 143–173 bpm (estimates only).')]],
['Benefits of physical activity',[
'Regular activity improves **heart and lung health**, **blood pressure**, **blood sugar control**, **bone density**, **muscle strength**, **sleep**, **mood** and **brain function**, and lowers the risk of **heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes, some cancers, depression** and **falls** in older adults. Benefits appear quickly—even a single session can improve mood and blood pressure.',
'**Physical inactivity** and **prolonged sitting** increase health risks. Breaking up long sitting periods with movement helps.',
"Physical activity also benefits academic performance, with research linking active students to better attention and classroom behavior.",
"Even short bouts of activity count toward weekly totals. Ten minutes of brisk walking three times a day adds up to the same 30 minutes as one continuous session.",
sort('Benefit of regular activity?',['Benefit','Not a benefit'],[['Lower blood pressure',0],['Higher risk of type 2 diabetes',1],['Better sleep',0],['Weaker bones',1],['Improved mood',0],['Reduced fall risk in older adults',0]])]],
['The FITT principle',[
'Plan activity with **FITT**: **Frequency** (how often), **Intensity** (how hard), **Time** (how long) and **Type** (what kind). For example, “Jog (type) at moderate intensity (intensity) for 30 minutes (time) four days per week (frequency).”',
'Adjust one FITT element at a time to progress safely, such as adding 5 minutes or one more day. FITT is useful when writing a measurable SMART goal.',
"FITT can describe any activity goal. A walking goal might be “walk briskly (type) at moderate intensity (intensity) for 20 minutes (time) five days a week (frequency).”",
order('Writing a FITT plan',['Choose the type of activity','Set frequency per week','Set intensity (moderate or vigorous)','Set time per session','Plan how to progress','Track sessions in a log'])]],
['Fitness assessments',[
'Fitness can be measured with simple tests: a **timed mile** or step test (cardiorespiratory endurance), **push-ups** or **curl-ups** (muscular endurance), a **sit-and-reach** (flexibility) and **body measurements** (body composition, interpreted carefully). Programs such as FitnessGram use standards for youth.',
'Repeating the same test under the same conditions shows progress—exactly the kind of measurable evidence a Healthy Living portfolio needs.',
"Choose tests that match your goal and record conditions—time of day, location and weather—so changes reflect your fitness rather than different circumstances.",
chk('Which test measures flexibility?',['Sit-and-reach','Timed mile','Push-ups','Step test'],'Range of motion.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Guidelines drill',{a:{text:'How many minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity should a 15-year-old get each day? (Practice item.)',choices:[['60 minutes or more','b','Correct.',true],['20 minutes','b','Youth need 60 minutes daily.',false]]},b:{text:'How many days per week should adults do muscle-strengthening activities?',choices:[['2 or more','c','Correct.',true],['Never','c','Adults need 2+ days.',false]]},c:{text:'You add the guidelines table to your study sheet.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Youth 6–17: 60+ minutes daily of moderate-to-vigorous activity with vigorous, muscle- and bone-strengthening activity 3+ days each; adults: 150–300 minutes moderate or 75–150 vigorous weekly plus strength 2+ days; older adults add balance; move more and sit less.','Health-related fitness: cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition; skill-related includes agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed.','Measure intensity with the talk test, estimated max heart rate (220 − age; moderate about 50–70%, vigorous about 70–85%) and RPE; plan with FITT and measure progress with repeatable fitness tests.'],terms:[['Cardiorespiratory endurance','The ability of the heart and lungs to supply oxygen during sustained activity.'],['FITT','Frequency, intensity, time and type of exercise.'],['Talk test','Judging intensity by how easily you can talk during activity.']],mcqs:[q('How much daily activity do youth ages 6–17 need?','60 minutes or more','15 minutes','2 hours minimum','None','Guideline.'),q('How much moderate activity per week do adults need?','150–300 minutes','30 minutes','1,000 minutes','10 minutes','Guideline.'),q('How often should adults do muscle strengthening?','2 or more days per week','Once a month','Never','Every hour','Guideline.'),q('What do older adults add?','Balance training','Nothing','Only stretching','Only swimming','Guideline.'),q('Estimated maximum heart rate is:','220 − age','200 + age','100 − age','Age × 2','Formula.'),q('Which measures muscular endurance?','Push-ups in a row','One heavy lift','Sit-and-reach','Body fat test','Component.'),q('In FITT, the I stands for:','Intensity','Interest','Interval','Injury','Principle.'),q('During moderate activity you can usually:','Talk but not sing','Sing easily','Not talk at all','Sleep','Talk test.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Physical Activity Guidelines, the components of fitness and how to plan and measure activity.',rubric:[['Guidelines',['60 minutes','150','300','75','muscle','bone','balance']],['Components',['cardiorespiratory','strength','endurance','flexibility','body composition']],['Intensity',['talk test','220','heart rate','moderate','vigorous','rpe']],['Planning and measuring',['fitt','frequency','intensity','time','type','test','progress']]],model:'The Physical Activity Guidelines for Americans recommend that children and adolescents ages 6 to 17 get at least 60 minutes of moderate-to-vigorous activity every day, including vigorous activity, muscle-strengthening and bone-strengthening activity on at least three days each week. Adults should get 150 to 300 minutes of moderate or 75 to 150 minutes of vigorous aerobic activity weekly plus muscle strengthening on two or more days, and older adults add balance training; overall, everyone should move more and sit less. Health-related fitness has five components: cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition, while skill-related fitness includes agility, balance, coordination, power, reaction time and speed. To measure intensity, I can use the talk test—during moderate activity I can talk but not sing—or estimate my maximum heart rate as 220 minus my age; at 16 that is about 204, so moderate intensity is roughly 102 to 143 beats per minute and vigorous about 143 to 173. To plan, I would use the FITT principle—for example, jogging at moderate intensity for 30 minutes four days a week—and progress by changing one element at a time, such as adding five minutes. To measure progress, I would repeat the same tests under the same conditions, such as a timed mile for cardiorespiratory endurance, push-ups for muscular endurance and a sit-and-reach for flexibility, recording results in a log that could serve as evidence in a Healthy Living portfolio.'},sources:[RULES,PAG,CDCPA]};

export const HL5={title:'Training principles and exercise safety',hook:'Getting fitter is not just about working harder—it is about working smarter and safer. Training principles explain how the body adapts to exercise, while safety practices prevent injuries that could derail a healthy living goal. NASM, the event sponsor, is known for evidence-based training, so expect principles and safety questions on the test.',objectives:['Apply overload, progression, specificity, recovery and reversibility.','Plan warm-ups, cool-downs and flexibility work.','Prevent common exercise injuries and heat illness.','Design a safe progression for a fitness goal.'],sections:[
['How the body adapts',[
'The **overload principle** states that the body must be challenged beyond its usual level to improve. **Progression** means increasing that challenge gradually—a common guideline is to increase training volume by about **10% per week** or less. **Specificity** means adaptations match the type of training: running improves running endurance more than swimming does.',
'**Recovery** allows the body to repair and adapt—rest days and sleep are part of training. **Reversibility** (“use it or lose it”) means fitness declines when training stops. **Individual differences** mean people respond differently to the same program.',
"The principles work together. A good program provides enough overload to improve, progresses gradually, matches the goal and includes recovery to prevent burnout and injury.",
"Rest does not always mean doing nothing. Active recovery, such as light walking or easy cycling, can promote blood flow and reduce stiffness between harder sessions.",
match('Principle and example',[['Overload','Lifting slightly heavier weights than usual'],['Progression','Adding 5 minutes to runs each week'],['Specificity','Practicing sprints to improve sprint speed'],['Recovery','Taking a rest day after hard training'],['Reversibility','Losing endurance after weeks off'],['Individual differences','Two friends improving at different rates']])]],
['Warm-up and cool-down',[
'A **warm-up** of about **5–10 minutes** of light aerobic activity and **dynamic movements** (leg swings, arm circles, walking lunges) raises body temperature and prepares muscles and joints. **Static stretching** (holding a stretch) is often best after exercise or as a separate flexibility session.',
'A **cool-down** of light activity helps heart rate return gradually toward resting levels, followed by stretching for flexibility.',
"Flexibility training several days a week maintains range of motion. Stretches are usually held 10 to 30 seconds without bouncing, to the point of mild tension, not pain.",
order('A safe workout',['Dynamic warm-up (5–10 minutes)','Main workout','Light activity cool-down','Static stretching','Hydrate and refuel'])]],
['Preventing injury',[
'Common causes of exercise injuries include doing **too much too soon**, poor **technique**, inadequate **rest**, improper **footwear** or equipment and ignoring **pain**. Prevention: progress gradually, learn proper form (ideally from a qualified coach or trainer), cross-train, wear proper shoes, use protective equipment and stop if pain is sharp or worsening.',
'Muscle soreness a day or two after new exercise is common (delayed-onset muscle soreness); sharp, persistent pain is a warning sign. Acute injuries are often managed initially with rest, ice, compression and elevation and evaluation by a health professional when needed.',
"Proper footwear matters: running shoes should fit well and be replaced when worn out, often every few hundred miles, since worn cushioning can contribute to injuries.",
sort('Injury risk or prevention?',['Increases injury risk','Prevents injury'],[['Doubling running distance in one week',0],['Learning proper squat form',1],['Running through sharp knee pain',0],['Wearing supportive athletic shoes',1],['Skipping all rest days for months',0],['Cross-training with swimming',1]])]],
['Heat and environmental safety',[
'Exercising in heat increases the risk of **dehydration**, **heat exhaustion** (heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, nausea) and **heat stroke** (high body temperature, confusion, possibly hot dry skin—a medical emergency requiring 911 and rapid cooling). Prevent heat illness by hydrating, wearing light clothing, avoiding the hottest hours and **acclimatizing** gradually.',
'In cold weather, dress in layers. Use sunscreen outdoors and choose safe routes and lighting.',
"Signs of heat exhaustion call for stopping activity, moving to a cool place, drinking fluids and resting. If symptoms worsen or confusion appears, seek emergency care.",
chk('A runner becomes confused with a very high body temperature on a hot day. What is this most likely?',['Heat stroke—a medical emergency; call 911 and cool rapidly','Normal fatigue','Delayed-onset muscle soreness','Dehydration only, no action needed'],'Heat stroke is life-threatening.')]],
['Strength training basics',[
'Strength training uses **resistance**—body weight, bands or weights—to build muscle strength and endurance. Key terms: **repetitions (reps)**, **sets** and **rest intervals**. Beginners often start with lighter loads and more reps to learn technique, then progress.',
'Train all **major muscle groups** (legs, hips, back, abdomen, chest, shoulders and arms). Youth strength training is safe and beneficial with proper supervision and technique. Avoid holding your breath; exhale during exertion.',
"Allow at least a day of rest for a muscle group between intense strength sessions, which gives muscles time to repair and grow stronger.",
"Core exercises such as planks strengthen the muscles that stabilize the spine, improving posture and supporting safe movement in other exercises.",
ex('Planning progression','A beginner does 2 sets of 10 body-weight squats (plan invented for practice). Plan safe progression over 4 weeks.',['Week 1: 2 sets of 10 with good form','Week 2: 2 sets of 12','Week 3: 3 sets of 12','Week 4: 3 sets of 12 holding light dumbbells','Progress only if form stays good and pain-free'],'Gradual overload with attention to technique (plan invented for practice).')]],
['Sleep, rest and recovery',[
'Sleep is when much of the body’s repair occurs. Teens need about **8–10 hours** and adults **7 or more**. Signs of **overtraining** include persistent fatigue, declining performance, frequent illness, irritability and trouble sleeping.',
'Plan **rest days** and lighter weeks, vary activities and listen to your body. Recovery is part of a healthy living plan, not a break from it.',
"Keeping a training log with notes on sleep, soreness and mood helps you spot overtraining early and adjust before problems develop.",
sort('Sign of overtraining?',['Possible overtraining','Healthy response'],[['Performance declining for weeks despite training',0],['Mild soreness after a new workout',1],['Frequent illness and irritability',0],['Feeling energized after rest days',1],['Trouble sleeping and persistent fatigue',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Training decisions',{a:{text:'You ran 10 miles this week. How much should you increase next week? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['About 1 mile (around 10%) or less','b','Correct.',true],['Double to 20 miles','b','Progress gradually.',false]]},b:{text:'Your knee has sharp pain during runs. What do you do?',choices:[['Stop running, rest and get evaluated by a health professional','c','Correct.',true],['Run through it','c','Sharp pain is a warning sign.',false]]},c:{text:'You adjust your plan safely.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Overload, progression (about 10% per week or less), specificity, recovery, reversibility and individual differences explain how training works.','Warm up with dynamic movement (5–10 minutes), cool down and stretch statically after; prevent injury with gradual progress, good technique, proper shoes, cross-training and rest; sharp pain is a warning sign.','Prevent heat illness (heat stroke is an emergency); train all major muscle groups with proper form and breathing; get enough sleep and watch for overtraining signs.'],terms:[['Overload principle','Challenging the body beyond its usual level to cause improvement.'],['Specificity','Adaptations match the type of training performed.'],['Overtraining','Excessive training without enough recovery, harming performance and health.']],mcqs:[q('Which principle says fitness declines when training stops?','Reversibility','Overload','Specificity','Progression','Principle.'),q('A common progression guideline is to increase weekly volume by about:','10% or less','50%','100%','200%','Progression.'),q('Which is best during a warm-up?','Dynamic movements','Long static stretches only','Heavy maximum lifts','Sitting still','Warm-up.'),q('Which is a sign of heat stroke?','Confusion and very high body temperature','Mild thirst','Slight soreness','Feeling cool','Emergency.'),q('Specificity means:','Training adaptations match the activity','Any exercise improves everything equally','Rest is unnecessary','Only stretching works','Principle.'),q('Which increases injury risk?','Doubling distance in one week','Learning proper form','Wearing proper shoes','Taking rest days','Safety.'),q('How much sleep do teens generally need?','About 8–10 hours','About 4 hours','About 12–14 hours','About 6 hours','Recovery.'),q('Which is a sign of overtraining?','Declining performance and persistent fatigue','Mild soreness after a new workout','Feeling rested','Improving times','Recovery.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain key training principles and how to exercise safely while working toward a fitness goal.',rubric:[['Principles',['overload','progression','10%','specificity','recovery','reversibility']],['Warm-up and cool-down',['dynamic','warm-up','cool-down','static','stretching']],['Injury and heat',['technique','shoes','pain','rest','heat stroke','hydrate','acclimatize']],['Strength and recovery',['reps','sets','major muscle groups','breathing','sleep','overtraining']]],model:'To improve fitness safely, I would apply the overload principle by challenging my body a little beyond its usual level and progress gradually, increasing training volume by about 10% or less per week—for example, from 10 miles of running one week to about 11 the next. Following specificity, I would train in the way I want to improve, such as running to lower my mile time, while recognizing that recovery days and sleep are when my body adapts, and that fitness declines if I stop training because of reversibility. Each workout would start with a five- to ten-minute dynamic warm-up of light jogging, leg swings and lunges and end with a cool-down and static stretching. To prevent injury, I would learn proper technique from a qualified coach, wear supportive shoes, cross-train with swimming and stop if I felt sharp or worsening pain, seeking evaluation from a health professional. In hot weather, I would hydrate, wear light clothing, avoid the hottest hours and acclimatize gradually, knowing that confusion and a very high body temperature signal heat stroke, a medical emergency requiring 911 and rapid cooling. For strength, I would train all major muscle groups two to three days a week, starting with body-weight squats and push-ups in two sets of ten with good form, exhaling during exertion and progressing only when form stays good. I would sleep eight to ten hours, schedule rest days and watch for overtraining signs such as persistent fatigue, frequent illness and declining performance.'},sources:[RULES,NASM,PAG]};
